﻿ useState — manage data that changes
  Return values in pair

import { useState } from "react"; 
function Counter() {
//Declare new state variable
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      Count: {count}
    </button>
  );
}

Here:
count = current value 
setCount = function to change it 
useState(0) = initial value is 0 2. useEffect — perform an action after rendering


import { useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Component loaded");
    }, []);

  return <h1>Hello</h1>;
}




Commonly used for API calls, timers, subscriptions, etc.

﻿ 3. useContext — share data between components


const UserContext = createContext();

function App() {
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value=“Neil">
    <Profile />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}



Then another component can access “Neil" without passing it through every component as props. 4. useRef — reference a DOM element or store a value



import { useRef } from "react";

function App() {
  const inputRef = useRef();

  return (
    <>
      <input ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={() => inputRef.current.focus()}>
        Focus
      </button>
    </>
  );
}

